### What is Floyd's cycle detection and entry-finding algorithm?

Floyd's algorithm solves two problems in one: detecting whether a cycle exists and finding the exact node where the cycle begins. It uses two pointers moving at different speeds and requires only O(1) extra space. This is the complete solution that combines both phases.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TxnRecord {
    public String txnId;
    public BigDecimal amount;
    public TxnRecord next;

    public TxnRecord(String txnId, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.txnId = txnId;
        this.amount = amount;
    }
}
```

### How does phase one (detection) work?

Start both `slow` and `fast` at the head. Loop while `fast` and `fast.next` are not null. Each iteration, advance slow by one and fast by two. If `slow == fast`, a cycle exists.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TxnRecord;

public class FloydCycleDetector {
    public static TxnRecord detectCycleStart(TxnRecord head) {
        TxnRecord slow = head;
        TxnRecord fast = head;

        // Phase 1: detect cycle
        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
            if (slow == fast) {
                // Phase 2: find entry
                TxnRecord entry = head;
                while (entry != slow) {
                    entry = entry.next;
                    slow = slow.next;
                }
                return entry; // cycle start node
            }
        }
        return null; // no cycle
    }
}
```

### How does phase two (entry finding) work?

After detection, reset one pointer to the head and keep the other at the meeting point. Advance both by one step at a time. They meet at the cycle entry node.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TxnRecord;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FloydDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TxnRecord a = new TxnRecord("TXN-A", new BigDecimal("1000"));
        TxnRecord b = new TxnRecord("TXN-B", new BigDecimal("2000"));
        TxnRecord c = new TxnRecord("TXN-C", new BigDecimal("3000"));
        TxnRecord d = new TxnRecord("TXN-D", new BigDecimal("4000"));
        TxnRecord e = new TxnRecord("TXN-E", new BigDecimal("5000"));
        a.next = b; b.next = c; c.next = d; d.next = e;
        e.next = c; // cycle back to C

        TxnRecord start = FloydCycleDetector.detectCycleStart(a);
        if (start != null) {
            System.out.println("Cycle starts at: " + start.txnId);
        } else {
            System.out.println("No cycle");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Cycle starts at: TXN-C
```

### Why are the pointers guaranteed to meet inside a cycle?

Once both pointers are inside the loop, the fast pointer gains one node on the slow pointer every step. The gap shrinks by one each iteration. If the gap is d, after d more steps it is zero and they collide. The gap cannot skip zero because it changes by exactly one.

```text
Inside a cycle of length L:
  Step 0: gap = d
  Step 1: gap = d - 1
  Step 2: gap = d - 2
  ...
  Step d: gap = 0 → collision
```

### Why does the phase-two walk find the entry?

Let `a` = distance from head to cycle start, `b` = distance from cycle start to meeting point, `L` = cycle length. Slow traveled `a + b`. Fast traveled `2(a + b)`. The difference `a + b` must be a multiple of `L`: `a + b = nL`, so `a = nL - b`. This means walking `a` steps from the head and `a` steps from the meeting point (which equals `nL - b` steps around the loop) both land on the cycle start.

```java
// The math in action:
// a = distance from head to cycle start
// b = distance from cycle start to meeting point
// L = cycle length
// slow traveled: a + b
// fast traveled: 2(a + b)
// difference: a + b = multiple of L
// therefore: a = nL - b
// walking a from head AND a from meeting → both reach cycle start
```

### How is this different from the hash-set approach?

The hash-set approach stores every visited node and returns the first repeat. It detects and finds the cycle start in one pass but uses O(n) space. Floyd's uses O(1) space but requires the mathematical insight.

| Feature | Floyd's Algorithm | Hash-Set |
|---|---|---|
| Time | O(n) | O(n) |
| Space | O(1) | O(n) |
| Detects cycle | Yes | Yes |
| Finds start | Yes (phase 2) | Yes (first repeat) |
| Understanding | Requires math proof | Straightforward |

### What are the edge cases?

Empty list, single node no cycle, single node self-loop, cycle starting at head, and very long tail with short cycle.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TxnRecord;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FloydEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Empty
        System.out.println("Empty: " + FloydCycleDetector.detectCycleStart(null));

        // Single node, no cycle
        TxnRecord single = new TxnRecord("T1", new BigDecimal("100"));
        System.out.println("Single no cycle: " + FloydCycleDetector.detectCycleStart(single));

        // Single node, self-loop
        single.next = single;
        System.out.println("Self-loop: " + FloydCycleDetector.detectCycleStart(single).txnId);
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: null
Single no cycle: null
Self-loop: T1
```

### What about applications beyond linked lists?

The tortoise-and-hare principle applies to any sequence `x, f(x), f(f(x)), ...` where `f` is a function. If the sequence enters a cycle, Floyd's detects it. The classic "find the duplicate number" problem models array values as function outputs: `f(i) = nums[i]`, and a duplicate creates a cycle.

```java
public static int findDuplicate(int[] nums) {
    int slow = nums[0], fast = nums[0];
    do {
        slow = nums[slow];
        fast = nums[nums[fast]];
    } while (slow != fast);

    int entry = nums[0];
    while (entry != slow) {
        entry = nums[entry];
        slow = nums[slow];
    }
    return entry;
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should be able to derive the phase-two proof from scratch. The key equation `a + b = nL` comes from the fact that fast travels twice as far as slow, and any extra distance must be whole loops. From there, `a = nL - b` immediately justifies the reset-and-walk technique.

In production at National Bank of India, Floyd's algorithm runs as a validation step before processing imported transaction chains, detecting and locating corrupt loop-backs in O(1) space.

```java
public static void validateAndRepair(TxnRecord head) {
    TxnRecord cycleStart = FloydCycleDetector.detectCycleStart(head);
    if (cycleStart != null) {
        // Find the node before the cycle start and break the link
        TxnRecord runner = cycleStart;
        while (runner.next != cycleStart) runner = runner.next;
        runner.next = null;
        System.out.println("Repaired cycle at " + cycleStart.txnId);
    }
}
```
